# SRG-APP-000345-AU-000400  -  CAT II
# The Central Log Server must automatically lock the account until the
# locked account is released by an administrator when three unsuccessful
# login attempts in 15 minutes are exceeded.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000345-AU-000400
group:           V-221925
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      documented
cci:             CCI-002238
control_rev5:    AC-7

contribution:    For accounts held in Splunk's own authentication system, Splunk enforces
                 the lockout itself: authentication.conf [splunk_auth] lockoutUsers turns
                 lockout on, lockoutAttempts sets how many unsuccessful logins may occur
                 before the account is locked, lockoutThresholdMins sets how long the
                 failed-attempt counter runs before resetting, and lockoutMins sets how
                 long the lockout is held.

governs:         authentication.conf - How users sign in, and the password rules applied to local accounts.

check:           authentication.conf [splunk_auth] lockoutUsers matches ^(1|[Tt]rue)$
check:           authentication.conf [splunk_auth] lockoutAttempts max 3
check:           authentication.conf [splunk_auth] lockoutAttempts min 1
check:           authentication.conf [splunk_auth] lockoutThresholdMins min 15
check:           authentication.conf [splunk_auth] lockoutThresholdMins max 120
# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
vendor:          authentication.conf [splunk_auth] lockoutAttempts https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/authentication.conf
vendor:          authentication.conf [splunk_auth] lockoutMins https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/authentication.conf
vendor:          authentication.conf [splunk_auth] lockoutThresholdMins https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/authentication.conf
vendor:          authentication.conf [splunk_auth] lockoutUsers https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/authentication.conf
# --- end vendor verification

external:        Splunk's native lockout always expires on its own -
                 authentication.conf.spec:526-528 caps lockoutMins at 1440 minutes - so the
                 product cannot hold an account locked until an administrator releases it.
                 The administrator-release portion of AC-7 b is carried by an
                 organizational procedure in which an administrator unlocks the account
                 through Splunk user management, and by the organization accepting the
                 automatic expiry of the configured lockout period.
external:        [splunk_auth] governs only accounts held in Splunk's native authentication
                 system. Where authType is LDAP, SAML or ProxySSO, the failed-attempt
                 counting and lockout are enforced by the directory service or identity
                 provider and are assessed against that component.
external:        authentication.conf.spec:517-519, 528-530, 539-541 and 550-552 state that
                 on search head cluster members the lockout state applies per member rather
                 than to the whole cluster, so cluster-wide enforcement depends on the
                 deployment topology.
external:        Holding a locked account until an administrator releases it, which
                 authentication.conf does not express: lockoutMins sets a duration whose
                 documented maximum is 1440 minutes, and no value means release by an
                 administrator only.

citation:        SRG-APP-000345-AU-000400 check text ('lock out the account until released
                 by an administrator when 3 consecutive invalid attempts during a 15 minute
                 period is exceeded'); product STIG SPLK-CL-000070 fix ('Modify the
                 following lines in the authentication.conf file under the [splunk_auth]:
                 lockoutUsers = True or 1') and check ('If the lockoutUsers is missing or
                 is configured to 0 or False, this is a finding');
                 authentication.conf.spec:437 ([splunk_auth]),
                 authentication.conf.spec:514-519 (lockoutUsers = <boolean>, default true),
                 authentication.conf.spec:532-541 (lockoutAttempts = <positive integer>,
                 unsuccessful attempts before lockout, must occur within
                 lockoutThresholdMins, minimum 1, maximum 64, default 5),
                 authentication.conf.spec:543-552 (lockoutThresholdMins = <positive
                 integer>, minutes that must pass from the first failed login before the
                 counter resets, minimum 1, maximum 120, default 5),
                 authentication.conf.spec:521-530 (lockoutMins = <positive integer>,
                 minutes a user is locked out, minimum 1, maximum 1440, default 30)
finding:         Splunk Enterprise does not lock native accounts on three failed logins in
                 15 minutes: authentication.conf [splunk_auth] lockoutUsers is missing or
                 set to 0 or False, lockoutAttempts is absent or greater than 3,
                 lockoutThresholdMins is absent or less than 15 so the failed-attempt
                 counter resets inside the measured period, or lockoutMins is not
                 explicitly configured.
